  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Nicknamed "little Corsica", Pointe des Sardinaux is a wild natural headland, a quiet spot for picnicking, sunbathing and discovering plants, seaweed, shells and crustaceans. You reach it via the pine-lined coast road towards Saint-Raphaël, just before La Nartelle beach — from the road it's easy to miss, and access is on foot only. The site holds two reminders of the past: a Second World War bunker, a relic of the 15 August 1944 Provence landings, and a Roman-era fish tank.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to Sainte-Maxime tourist office, the land portion of Pointe des Sardinaux is one of only two spots on the town's coastline where dogs are allowed year-round — except in July and August, when access is closed to them. The southern tip of the headland, which is the designated swimming area, remains off-limits to dogs year-round, as on the rest of the town's beaches. Owned by the Var department and managed by the town, the headland is part of the coastal path.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: come outside July-August to enjoy the site with your dog — in peak summer, head instead to the <a href="/en/fiche-sainte-maxime">Pont du Préconil dog beach</a>, open year-round without interruption.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>La Nartelle beach is just beyond, to the east, and Sainte-Maxime's town centre with the <a href="/en/fiche-sainte-maxime">Pont du Préconil dog beach</a> is a few minutes' drive away.</p>
    </div>

